WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.400
Thinking mode asks a model to produce or use a longer reasoning process before the final answer.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:14.401
That can help some language problems, but perception-heavy tasks first require accurate grounding in the pixels.

00:00:14.401 --> 00:00:20.201
More reasoning cannot recover text or objects the visual stage failed to capture.

00:00:20.201 --> 00:00:30.802
The referenced benchmark found that thinking mode could hurt some vision tasks through added latency, instability, or language reasoning that drifted away from visible evidence.

00:00:30.802 --> 00:00:36.602
Treat that as a testable observation, not a permanent rule for every model.

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:45.603
For direct OCR, object listing, simple description, or routine event classification, start with thinking disabled when the model supports that option.

00:00:45.603 --> 00:00:49.403
This can reduce output length and improve throughput.

00:00:49.403 --> 00:00:54.004
Compare accuracy and completion rather than assuming shorter is better.

00:00:54.004 --> 00:01:01.004
Reasoning may help after perception for charts, diagrams, multi-step visual questions, or combining several extracted facts.

00:01:01.004 --> 00:01:08.005
A useful workflow separates the steps: first report visible evidence, then reason from the reported evidence.

00:01:08.005 --> 00:01:13.405
Prompts should ask the model to state uncertainty and avoid unsupported claims.

00:01:13.405 --> 00:01:18.405
Review whether the explanation cites visible labels, positions, values, or objects.

00:01:18.405 --> 00:01:21.405
Fluent internal reasoning is not verification.

00:01:21.405 --> 00:01:25.606
Benchmark both modes with the same images and scoring.

00:01:25.606 --> 00:01:29.806
Measure grounding accuracy, latency, completion, consistency, and resource use.

00:01:29.806 --> 00:01:36.406
Enable thinking only when it produces a repeatable benefit for the actual task and hardware.
